---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Forms & operations software for nonprofits"
  description="Jotform, Airtable, and the operational glue of a fundraising stack — intake forms, registrations, and lightweight databases, with real nonprofit pricing."
  section="forms-ops">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Forms & operations' }]} />
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">Category guide</p>
      <h1>Forms &amp; other operations</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The unglamorous layer: program applications, volunteer intake, event
        registrations, scholarship forms, and the small databases that hold a
        shop together. It's the cheapest category in the stack — Jotform starts
        free and its paid plans run half price for nonprofits — and the one that
        quietly eats the most staff time when it's missing.
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        Buy a real form builder the day a program application outgrows email.
        <strong>Jotform</strong> is the practical default: a free tier (5 forms,
        100 submissions/month), paid plans from about $34/mo, and a standing
        <strong>50% nonprofit discount</strong> that makes the paid tiers cheap
        enough to stop debating. It handles conditional logic, file uploads,
        payments, and HIPAA-adjacent needs that break lesser tools.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Airtable</strong> fills a different hole: the structured lists
        that aren't donors — grant deadlines, program rosters, in-kind
        inventory. Its free tier carries most small shops. The discipline that
        matters more than the tool: anything about a <em>donor</em> belongs in
        the CRM, not in a side spreadsheet — side databases are where gift
        history goes to die.
      </p>

      <h2>The one mistake to avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Don't collect donations through a generic form builder if you can help
        it. Form tools can take payments, but dedicated
        <a href="/donation-processing/">donation platforms</a> convert better,
        handle receipts properly, and manage recurring gifts. Use forms for
        operations; use giving tools for gifts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="forms-ops" />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Forms tooling appears across every tier — see
        <a href="/stacks/250k-1m/">the $250k–$1M stack</a> for where it first
        earns a paid line.
      </p>
      <AffiliateDisclosure />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
